<h2 id="troubleshooting-mgmtdb"><strong>Troubleshooting MGMTDB</strong></h2>
<h3 id="1-connectivity-and-daemon-issues"><strong>1. Connectivity and Daemon Issues</strong></h3>
<p>MGMTDB relies on two daemons:</p>
<ul>
  <li><code class="highlighter-rouge">osysmond</code> (runs on every node, collects metrics).</li>
  <li><code class="highlighter-rouge">ologgerd</code> (runs on the master node, writes to MGMTDB).</li>
</ul>

<h4 id="diagnostic-commands"><strong>Diagnostic Commands</strong></h4>
<div class="language-bash highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><span class="c"># Check daemon status</span>
crsctl <span class="nb">stat </span>res ora.crf <span class="nt">-init</span> <span class="nt">-t</span>

<span class="c"># Identify the master node (where ologgerd runs)</span>
oclumon manage <span class="nt">-get</span> master

<span class="c"># Enable debug logging (level 3)</span>
oclumon debug log osysmond allcomp:3  <span class="c"># Local node</span>
oclumon debug log ologgerd allcomp:3  <span class="c"># Master node</span>
</code></pre></div></div>
<blockquote>
  <p><strong>Log Location</strong>: <code class="highlighter-rouge">$GRID_HOME/log/&lt;hostname&gt;/crf/</code></p>
</blockquote>

<hr />

<h3 id="2-disk-space-management"><strong>2. Disk Space Management</strong></h3>
<p>MGMTDB can fill up its ASM Disk Group. Mitigation steps:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Check space usage</strong>:
    <div class="language-sql highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><span class="c1">-- Connect to -MGMTDB as sysdba</span>
<span class="k">SELECT</span> <span class="n">file_name</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">bytes</span><span class="o">/</span><span class="mi">1024</span><span class="o">/</span><span class="mi">1024</span> <span class="n">MB</span> <span class="k">FROM</span> <span class="n">dba_data_files</span><span class="p">;</span>
</code></pre></div>    </div>
  </li>
  <li><strong>Add space to the Disk Group</strong> (e.g., <code class="highlighter-rouge">+MGMT</code>).</li>
  <li><strong>Reduce retention</strong> (if space is constrained):
    <div class="language-bash highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code>oclumon manage <span class="nt">-repos</span> changereposize 86400  <span class="c"># 24 hours</span>
</code></pre></div>    </div>
  </li>
</ol>

<hr />

<h3 id="3-recreating-mgmtdb-last-resort"><strong>3. Recreating MGMTDB (Last Resort)</strong></h3>
<p>If the repository is corrupted, use Oracle’s tools to recreate it:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Oracle 19c+</strong>: <code class="highlighter-rouge">mgmtca</code> (Management Database Configuration Assistant).</li>
  <li><strong>Oracle 12c</strong>: Follow MOS Note <strong>1589394.1</strong> for scripts.</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2 id="best-practices"><strong>Best Practices</strong></h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Storage</strong>: Use a dedicated ASM Disk Group (<code class="highlighter-rouge">+MGMT</code>) for MGMTDB.</li>
  <li><strong>Retention</strong>: Adjust <code class="highlighter-rouge">oclumon</code> retention based on your diagnostic needs (default 24h is often too short).</li>
  <li><strong>Monitoring</strong>: Integrate MGMTDB metrics into your observability stack (e.g., Grafana, Prometheus).</li>
  <li><strong>Backups</strong>: While MGMTDB is not critical for cluster operation, back up its datafiles if you rely on historical diagnostics.</li>
  <li><strong>Security</strong>: Restrict access to MGMTDB; it contains sensitive cluster metadata.</li>
</ol>

